{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "src-05-simplifying-surds-radical-expressions",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Simplifying Surds and Radical Expressions",
    "description": "Pull square factors out of radicals, combine like surds, and spot radical expressions that collapse by identity.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start with hidden square factors, then combine like surds, keep outside coefficients straight, simplify radical products and quotients, use conjugate products, convert supplied approximations only after simplification, recognise two-term radical squares, and finish with the chapter’s three-surd square pattern.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach surd simplification through hidden perfect-square factors: √(ab)=√a√b, then combine only like surds after simplification. Cover coefficients, addition and subtraction of like surds, products and quotients under roots, conjugate products that become a difference of squares, and expressions built from a supplied approximation such as √2 or √3. Teach recognition of a radical that is itself a perfect square, for example a form matching (√a+√b)^2. End with the chapter-style three-surd square pattern behind √(a+b+c+2√ab+2√bc+2√ca). Explicitly warn that √(a+b) is not generally √a+√b.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 31"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn to make ugly square roots smaller before doing any arithmetic.\n\nMost surd questions become easy when you spot a perfect-square factor or a familiar square hiding inside the radical.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A surd often hides a perfect square.\n\n√72 looks awkward, but 72 = 36 × 2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So:\n\n√72 = √36 × √2 = 6√2\n\nPull the perfect-square part out; leave the non-square part under the root.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
